# Ordering assertions must be POSITION-AWARE. The one ordering guard this project
# had was a pair of substring tests, and an audit relocated the entire compute-
# and-gate block to sit AFTER the deletions — reproducing the exact defect the
# assertion existed to prevent, with the assertion green.
def _pos(hay, needle):
    i = hay.find(needle)
    return i if i >= 0 else 10 ** 9


_sweep = _pos(aud, "## Step 6c — The sweep")
_gate = _pos(aud, "Compute every Run Invariant FIRST")
_close = _pos(aud, "## Step 7 — Close")
check(
    "ordering: the invariant gate is positioned before the sweep it guards",
    _gate < _sweep + len("## Step 6c — The sweep") + 4000 and _gate > _sweep,
    "the gate must sit inside Step 6c, ahead of the removal table — a substring "
    "test cannot see that the block was moved",
)
check(
    "ordering: the sweep is sequenced before close",
    _sweep < _close,
    "Step 6c must precede Step 7; an audit moved the sweep after close and every "
    "substring assertion still passed",
)

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Discharge — the drain for rows no command owns.
#
# `odyssey-alive` closed a run with six deferred rows of which FOUR were malformed
# against deferred.md's own legitimacy rule: two named the run's own command
# (`/workforce sweep`), two named the run's own newly hired org ("dispatching
# `automation-engineer`"). INV-DEFERRED balanced perfectly across all six, because
# it audits the ARITHMETIC of a queue and never the LEGITIMACY of a row.
#
# Position matters more than presence here. A NOT UPHELD invariant aborts the
# sweep and discharge is what repairs one, so discharge placed after the sweep is
# a gate firing strictly after the thing it was meant to stop — the exact defect
# invariants.md records about its own first version.
_dsc = read("workforce/references/procedures/discharge.md")
_discharge = _pos(aud, "## Step 6b — Discharge")
check(
    "ordering: discharge is sequenced before the sweep it unblocks",
    _discharge < _sweep,
    "Step 6b must precede Step 6c. Discharge repairs the NOT UPHELD rows that abort "
    "the sweep; placed after it, it repairs a gate that already refused and the run "
    "ends unfinished one step later than before",
)
check(
    "discharge: a refusal cites a shipped rule at path:line",
    "cites a shipped rule at `path:line`" in _dsc,
    "without it a refusal that merely SOUNDS like a rule closes a row. The two "
    "measured refusals were 'a behavior change beyond what an audit may make "
    "unasked' (in no shipped file) and a real rule about `audit` cited by a "
    "command that is not `audit`",
)
check(
    "discharge: a user decision is decided, never queued",
    "DECIDED" in _dsc
    and "no longer a queueable category" in _dsc
    and "user decision" not in dfr.split("Two, and there used to be three")[0].split(
        "| Legitimate | Example |"
    )[-1],
    "deferred.md must not list a user decision as legitimately queueable — the "
    "queue was where decisions went to not be made",
)
check(
    "discharge: this run's own org is not a discharger either",
    "never discharged by this run's own ORG" in dfr
    and "delays **dispatch by name**, not the work" in dfr,
    "the blocking rule named 'the command that wrote it'; the same run's newly "
    "hired employees are the same evasion one level down, and two of six measured "
    "rows read exactly that way",
)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The mechanism layer — skills own mechanism, employees own judgment.
#
# The one real audit classified 31 skills PROMOTE and reduced ZERO, then reported
# that "38 skills' judgment cannot fit into nine handbooks." That measured the
# wrong thing: the handbook was never where the mechanism was going. The user's
# directive makes conversion SEPARATE a skill rather than absorb one, and these
# assertions hold the three parts that can silently not happen — the reduction,
# its verification, and the conditional mark.
_ct2 = read("workforce/references/conversion-taxonomy.md")
_hire2 = read("workforce/references/procedures/hire.md")
# Found 2026-08-04 by tracing a new-feature ask through /org rung 10(b) end to
# end. `hire.md` mentioned data skills ZERO times while Step 2 placed "its
# records" and Step 5 recorded an EMP — so the path that hands a new capability's
# verbatim words to `hire` produced a handbook naming records nothing created.
# audit Step 6 lists data skills in its order; this path never did. The producer
# existed (`/workforce handbook <data-skill>`) and was wired into one path only.
# Asked whether the routines that BUILD new workflows are the ones that OPTIMIZE
